# LOG .gitignore
#
# LOG is a personal ROM hack. The repository carries the code that BUILDS the hack, never the game data it
# builds from or produces. Nothing here is a copyright notice: it is the list of what must never be pushed.
#
# CLAUDE.md names builds\, data\ and zhack\. The real rule is wider, so this file is the rule:
# archive\ and scratch\ hold 446 ROM, save and state files between them, 3.3 GB and 1.3 GB.

# ===== folders that hold game data =====
# data\      the owner's own ROM dumps, rips and references. Never redistributable, and never written to.
# builds\    every build ROM, with its .sav and savestates.
# zhack\     another modder's hack: the source ROM, its IPS patch, and the study sheets taken from it.
# archive\   dated copies of old builds, art rounds and snapshots. 3.3 GB, and full of ROMs.
# scratch\   throwaway: test ROMs, sprite exports, ROM copies, the Python environment, Ghidra projects. 1.3 GB.
builds/
data/
zhack/
archive/
scratch/

# ===== game data by extension, anywhere =====
# Belt and braces. A ROM, a save or a savestate that lands outside the folders above is still barred.
# The Z Hack ROM proved the need: it sat in docs\, where no folder rule reached it.
*.gba
*.gb
*.gbc
*.sav
*.srm
*.ss[0-9]
*.ips
*.bps
*.ups

# BizHawk writes these two names during a capture run (scripts\permanent\emu\capture.py).
*.SaveRAM
*.State

# ===== generated output =====
# scripts\one-time\  one dated folder per study: 205 MB of probe listings, ROM copies and a Ghidra log.
#                    The study reports in docs\tasks\ carry the findings, which is what a reader needs.
# test-data\         the save files the tests read. They are game data.
scripts/one-time/
test-data/

# ===== Python and editor =====
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
LOG.code-workspace

# ===== art: finished work only =====
# The zhack\ layout is the model: a finalized sprite list, not a working folder. Scratch art, working rounds,
# mockup frames, review GIFs and ripped vanilla sheets never go up (owner, 2026-10-31).
#
# Three things are kept, and nothing else:
#   1. build inputs      the patch modules read these, so a clone cannot build the ROM without them
#   2. finished art      the finalized portraits and the master swatch
#   3. art\tables\       palette.json and portraits.json, read by art_inject
#
# Gohan's gi sets are NOT finished art. They are here only because gohan_gi is line 1 of the main build
# list. Remove the four gi lines below to drop them, and a clean clone then cannot build r40.
art/*

# --- build inputs ---
!art/tables/

# --- finished portraits: the 6 finals, the manifest and the owner's source drawing. Not work\ or remaster\ ---
!art/portrait-sources/
art/portrait-sources/*
!art/portrait-sources/*.png
!art/portrait-sources/manifest.json
!art/portrait-sources/src/

# --- the master swatch ---
!art/base-audit/
art/base-audit/*
!art/base-audit/gi_master_swatch.png

# --- build inputs that live at the top of art\ or in an art subfolder ---
# Proven by building the exported index, not by reading the code: each line below is a file the main
# build list actually opens. ssj2_form reads the two HUD icon blobs; superperfect_cell reads cell_aura.
!art/hud_z2_usable.bin
!art/hud_z2_dim.bin
!art/cell_aura/
art/cell_aura/*
!art/cell_aura/scale_15/

# The four gi art folders left on 2026-10-31, when the golden master stopped needing them.
# Golden builds from vanilla pixels (gohan_gi_vanilla). The gi art is dev-only and stays on disk.
# A clone can build the golden master. It cannot build dev, which is the point.

# ===== local working files, not part of the hack =====
# CLAUDE.md drives the local tooling. It is not something a modder cloning this needs, and the repository
# should not carry assistant-specific files. It stays on disk and out of the repository.
CLAUDE.md

# Another modder's hack, kept for clean-room study only. Never published, never copied into our art.
kai/

# The ledger is my day-by-day work diary, not something a modder cloning this needs.
# It stays on disk and keeps being written; it just does not go to the repository.
docs/ledger/
